  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 216 PM MST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nThe cold front has passed through this morning, bringing widespread \nnortherly winds of 40-55 mph across the plains this morning and \nearly afternoon. With boundary layer winds remaining at 40-50 kts \nthrough the rest of the day, surface wind gusts of 40-55 mph, with \nlocalized gusts of 60-65 mph, will continue through the overnight \nhours and possibly through early Tuesday morning. The I-25 Corridor \nand Denver metro should remain sheltered from the strongest winds, \nbut still expect gusts of 20-30 mph periodically. \n\nCurrent radar imagery shows ongoing snow showers across the high \nterrain and rainfall/isolated thunderstorms over the plains. \nForecast remains generally on track, although have lowered expected \nsnowfall amounts for the mountains slightly, as they have struggled \nto produce decent snowfall this morning. Observations have only \nshown a dusting to 1\" with localized amounts up to 2\". However, we \nshould start seeing more snowfall this afternoon and tonight as the \nbest QG lift from the upper level low will move through. Overall, \nexpect 2-6\" of total snowfall, with localized higher amounts up to \n9\" confined to the highest elevations. For the plains, scattered \nrain showers will continue with generally 0.1-0.4\" of QPF expected. \nUpsloping will help the Palmer Divide receiver higher rainfall \namounts than Denver metro and northern plains (the northern I-25 \nCorridor unfortunately expected to remain mostly dry due to \ndownsloping from Cheyenne Ridge). However, CAMs have honed in on \nportions of the eastern plains (eastern Adams, western Washington \nand northern Lincoln counties area) receiving localized higher \namounts of rainfall due to upsloping, with DESI probabilities \nshowing a 40-50% of rainfall amounts >0.50\" by Tuesday morning. In \naddition, it is not out of the realm of possibilities that the \nPalmer Divide and the aforementioned area could see rain/snow mix \ntonight with the upslope enhancement and near-freezing overnight \ntemperatures. Modeled Skew-T soundings show a moist vertical profile \nbelow O dg C, with a very shallow \"warm\" layer at the surface that \nwill likely melt most of the snowfall. Even if snow makes it to the \nsurface, warm ground temperatures should keep from any \naccumulations. \n\nExpect most of the mountain snow and rain showers across the plains \nto be finished by Tuesday morning. However, we will still be breezy \nbehind the exiting system. Northerly wind gusts of 20-30 mph is \npossible across the plains. Temperatures will be a bit warmer as \nupper level ridging returns, with forecasted highs in the 50s across \nthe plains. By Wednesday, a weak upper level trough will race across \nColorado which will bring increased chances for mountain snow and \nrain showers across the plains. As of right now, the track of the \nsystem favors precipitation for our southern mountains (I-70 \nmountain corridor and south), however snowfall amounts will be \nlight. \n\nBroad upper level ridging will allow near-average temperatures \nthrough the weekend and mostly dry conditions. Ensemble solutions do \nhint at an upper level low from the Pacific Northwest tracking \ntowards the northern Great Plains. As of right now, this system \nlooks to be too far northeast to bring precipitation to our forecast \narea. However, with the increased flow aloft, we could see some \nbreezy winds across the eastern plains by the end of the weekend.",
